This project is labeled Beta_Robotics_2024-2025 because it is
where I will be doing programming for Beta alongside helping you as well.  As I'm sure you have
noticed, each new programmer has their own 'branch'.  The concept of git, branches, commits, etc
may be confusing at first, but it will all make sense with time.  What's important is that you know
how to manage your branch specifically.  The features you will be utilizing in git are commiting,
pushing, and pulling code.  Basically when you write code in this project it, at first, will only
exist on your computer.  To use collaborative programming, which I HIGHLY recommend, you need to put
your code online in a git repository.  I have already made the repository for this project for you
which can be found at "https://github.com/Jacob-Mayhue/Beta_Robotics_2024-2025.git".  To put your
code online you need to use the 'commit' command and the 'push' command.

To use these commands click the icon that looks like a horizontal line with a circle in the middle
that can be found under the file tab near the top left of your screen, or use the Alt+0 shortcut. If
you haven't changed anything yet then this tab will mostly be blank, but after you edit the project
in some way it should change.  You should see a couple check boxes inside of a 'Changes' dropdown and
a terminal that says "Commit Message" when empty.  The first thing you want to do is check the checkbox
attached to 'changes' this will select all the altered code to be commited to github.  After that
write a SHORT message in the terminal that describes what you did with these changes.  This message
should be reasonable and relevant to your changes, please do not write any silly or dumb commit
messages as this is practice for the real thing.  This message should also be understandable to
someone other than yourself.  After typing the message click "Commit and Push..." at the bottom of
the terminal.  In the bottom right you will most likely get a message about warnings/errors in your
code, don't worry this is completely normal and will almost always occur.  Just tell it to commit
and push anyways.  After this click the push button at the bottom of the pop-up window.  After that
you should be done!  Congrats on your first commit!  Please note you should only be altering code on
YOUR branch unless you have permission from the branches owner, please do not mess with anyone
else's code without permission, and UNDER NO CIRCUMSTANCES should you ever alter anything on
Jacob's-Branch.  This code will be used for the actual Beta robot and I do not want it to be altered.
If you do alter my code I have no problem deleting your branch and removing you from this repository.

The other git command you may use is the 'pull' command.  This takes the online version of the project
and puts it on your computer.  You can really just think of it as updating you project.  To use this
command simply press Control+T and click okay (Pull is a lot easier than commits :) ).

Now that you understand the basics of using GitHub you're ready to start programming.  Throughout
the lessons/tasks I've set up for you you'll see your instructions in a green text with a TODO
tag.  Follow these instructions and hints I leave and move on to the next stage after I've checked
you off.  After you do any coding you should always commit and push your code so that if I'm not
around I can still access your code from the online repository.  After reviewing your code I'll give
feedback and suggestions (Duncan can also check/review your code as well).  I may periodically check
your progress on my own and leave behind comments with the FIXME
tag.  This doesn't necessarily mean something is wrong with your code rather it's another perspective
to consider.

Finally, if you have any questions on ANYTHING coding related ask Duncan or I.  Additionally if you
really can't figure something out and neither of us are around feel free to check my branch and see
how I personally do things.  Remember to not change any code, just look please.  Don't forget that
you will need to pull my changes to see my current code status (I will try my best to commit and push
after each day).  Start with the below tagged instructions to begin.


